{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The 'strictly more total internal redundancy' of the large service is effectively neutralized for correlated failure if the decentralization is empirically achieved via hardware/software diversity, because a large service operates under a single deployment philosophy that becomes a single point of correlated failure (e.g., a uniform CI/CD pipeline or a uniform firmware version). Thus, the small services' independence of operation—lacking a shared brain—is the dominant factor in surviving shared causes.","counterconditions":["If the large service can be shown to possess operational independence of redundant components equivalent to the decentralization (e.g., separate teams, disjoint software stacks, disjoint supply chains), the claim weakens.","If the decentralized network converges on a single shared implementation (e.g., all small operators use the same cloud provider or the same open-source library), the dominant factor disappears and the large service's planned redundancy wins."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Centralized redundancy is planned: it eliminates independent failure modes by design but also eliminates independent *survival* modes. A single shared cause (e.g., a bad configuration push, a common supply-chain compromise) propagates through the large service's redundant components precisely because they are not independent of each other in operation. A decentralized network has no single point of operational control; even if components are individually less reliable, the probability that a shared cause reaches all of them is bounded by the probability of coincident operational alignment, which is lower than the near-certainty of alignment inside a single large service.","optional_refs":["bcb80ce80ffb951b467521526aee8ee743f3b17aabb683b40c125487920a30c7"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Decentralized networks are more vulnerable to the exact class of correlated failures they are advertised as solving, because they cannot enforce or verify the independence they rely on. The apparent uncorrelatedness of small operators is an epistemological artifact of small samples and short observation windows; a large service can engineer and test independence of its redundancy, while a decentralized network cannot even measure it.","counterconditions":["If a decentralized network had strong empirical evidence of long-run uncorrelated failure modes across its operators (e.g., a multi-year dataset showing no coincident failures across unrelated operators), the claim would weaken.","If large services were shown to have a poor historical record of actually verifying independence (e.g., widespread simultaneous failures of redundant components within large services), the claim would weaken."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The large service has a closed dependency graph for its redundancy: it can enumerate the components, test for shared dependencies, and actively inject faults to verify isolation. A decentralized network has an open dependency graph: operators independently choose software, hardware, and upstream providers. The network cannot observe or control these choices, so it cannot detect hidden common dependencies (e.g., all operators happen to rely on the same cloud provider, the same TLS library, or the same upstream transit). The very autonomy that is claimed as resilience is the mechanism that prevents the detection of correlated risk.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","eb4515296f5292fab30875a85afa5f21db8fbdea9615468bb2d07d0cc19c62e2","bcb80ce80ffb951b467521526aee8ee743f3b17aabb683b40c125487920a30c7"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.72},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The distinction between 'truly independent' and 'merely appears uncorrelated until a shared cause is found' is not resolvable by any finite evidence, because any observed lack of correlation can be attributed to a sample that does not include the shared cause yet. Therefore, the debate is underdetermined: the choice between centralized redundancy and decentralization should be made on other grounds (e.g., cost, latency, governance), because the key evidentiary question is unanswerable.","counterconditions":["If a rigorous, closed-form model of all possible shared causes were available and could be empirically tested against, the claim of underdetermination would weaken.","If the standard of evidence were relaxed to 'independence with respect to a defined, bounded class of causes', the question becomes resolvable and the claim no longer applies."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The evidentiary standard requires proving a negative (absence of a shared cause) over a space of possible shared causes that is open-ended and includes unknown unknowns (e.g., a new vulnerability class, a novel social phenomenon, an unforeseen regulatory action). No finite observation can rule out all future shared causes. Any apparent independence is conditional on the set of shared causes considered so far; a new shared cause can always be discovered that collapses the observed independence. The question is therefore not merely empirical but epistemological: the kind of evidence requested cannot exist in principle.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.25},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The 'small, independently operated services' premise is often a fiction: in practice, decentralization collapses into a de facto centralization on a small number of shared infrastructure providers (e.g., cloud, DNS, CDNs). Therefore, the comparison is false; the decentralized network inherits the correlated failure modes of the underlying infrastructure while losing the large service's ability to engineer around them.","counterconditions":["If small operators were shown to use diverse infrastructure (e.g., different cloud providers, on-premise hardware, different regions) in a way that is uncorrelated, the claim would weaken.","If large services were shown to also depend on the same infrastructure providers (e.g., cloud regions) and to lack the resources or will to build diversity, the claim would weaken."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Decentralization at the application layer does not imply decentralization at the infrastructure layer. Small operators, seeking efficiency, converge on the same low-cost, high-availability infrastructure. This creates a hidden dependency: the network's apparent independence is an artifact of application-layer diversity, but its survival is conditional on the uptime of a small number of infrastructure providers. A large service, by contrast, can afford to build redundant, diverse infrastructure or to pay for diversity; a small operator cannot.","optional_refs":["eb4515296f5292fab30875a85afa5f21db8fbdea9615468bb2d07d0cc19c62e2","09a3c8106b138005252dd72e7642b00f65aa891fecff6e3fa95b043a5b98435b","d4bb4efc978c30b85d3df3879792f69d98bbdf3ece4bfbfec3b2e3ae89ffcc92"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.78},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A large service with strictly more total internal redundancy is more resilient to correlated failure than a decentralized network of small services, provided the large service has the resources to diversity its redundant components across independent supply chains, geographies, and software implementations. The problem's premise that the large service has 'strictly more total internal redundancy' implies it can afford redundancy in the dimensions that matter for correlation, while small services cannot.","counterconditions":["If the large service's redundancy is strictly homogeneous (e.g., many copies of the same software on the same hardware), the claim fails.","If the small services, through coordination, achieve diversity along the relevant dimensions (e.g., a protocol that requires diverse implementations), the claim weakens."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Correlated failure is a function of shared dependencies. The large service, having more redundancy, can diversify not only the number of components but the *kinds* of components, supply chains, and operational procedures. A small service has fewer resources and cannot afford this diversity; it has a single stack, a single provider, a single team. The large service can engineer independence along the dimensions that would otherwise be shared causes; the small services have independence along dimensions that do not matter (e.g., ownership) but dependence along dimensions that do (e.g., infrastructure).","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","09a3c8106b138005252dd72e7642b00f65aa891fecff6e3fa95b043a5b98435b"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Evidence that would distinguish truly independent failure modes from merely uncorrelated ones is the existence of a failure that affected a large service's redundant components but did not affect small operators, or vice versa, under the same external stress. Specifically, a 'common-mode failure' within the large service that left the decentralized network intact would be strong evidence for the claim; the absence of such events in the record would be evidence against.","counterconditions":["If the large service and the decentralized network are never observed under the same external stress, the evidence cannot be collected.","If the two systems are so different in design that no common stress is meaningful, the comparison is invalid."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The key empirical question is whether a shared cause can propagate through the large service's redundancy but not through the decentralized network. If the large service's redundant components fail together under a stress that the decentralized network survives, the large service's redundancy was not independent. If both fail, the network was not independent either. The discriminating evidence is therefore asymmetric: events that break one but not the other under shared stress.","optional_refs":["8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3","eb4515296f5292fab30875a85afa5f21db8fbdea9615468bb2d07d0cc19c62e2"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45}],"simulation_proposals":[]}